(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Achieving prompt ventricular rate control in the setting of acute heart failure and atrial) Tj
T*
(fibrillation may accelerate hemodynamic recovery and symptom resolution. Forty patients) Tj
T*
(with acute decompensated heart failure, reduced ejection fraction, and uncontrolled atrial) Tj
T*
(fibrillation \(heart rate >130\) were randomly assigned to intensive rate control using) Tj
T*
(combination beta-blockade or to standard-care rate control. The intensive-control group) Tj
T*
(achieved target heart rate significantly faster, with median time to goal approximately) Tj
T*
(1.5 hours versus 4 hours in the standard group. At the 2-hour mark, intensive-control) Tj
T*
(participants demonstrated greater absolute heart rate reduction \(approximately 40%) Tj
T*
(decrease versus 23% in standard care\). Clinical endpoints including dyspnea improvement) Tj
T*
(and resolution of pulmonary congestion were more rapidly attained in the intensive-control) Tj
T*
(arm. Blood pressure remained stable in both groups without excessive hypotensive episodes.) Tj
T*
(At 24 hours, hemodynamic parameters converged between groups. Intensive rate-control) Tj
T*
(strategies offer early symptomatic benefit in acute heart failure complicated by rapid) Tj
T*
(atrial fibrillation.) Tj
